### 11.1 Four map presentation scales

| Map scale | Displays                                                              | Purpose                |
| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| World     | Continents, major territories, oceans and large geographical features | Long-distance planning |
| Region    | Local terrain, settlements, roads and landmarks                       | Regional exploration   |
| Local     | Nearby trails, structures, entrances and points of interest           | Immediate navigation   |
| Interior  | Dungeons, caves, labyrinths and underground connections               | Indoor exploration     |

Each scale should have its own visibility rules. Showing every individual tree, road, and landmark on the global map would make it unreadable.

### 11.2 Zoom-dependent rendering

Implement map detail levels:

* Global view: simplified region shapes and major landmarks.

* Continental view: major terrain features and settlement symbols.

* Regional view: roads, rivers, villages, ruins, and local geography.

* Local view: detailed exploration markers and discovered paths.

Use a consistent symbol language across all zoom levels.

### 11.3 Map rendering architecture

Recommended Unity implementation:

* A dedicated map camera or render pipeline.

* A separate map presentation layer.

* Region geometry generated from canonical data.

* Map icons managed independently of world objects.

* Zoom-based icon scaling.

* Icon clustering at low zoom.

* Visibility filters for discovered content.

* Optional topographic overlays.

* Separate map layers for surface and underground.

* UI virtualization for large numbers of markers.

Do not instantiate thousands of individual UI objects every time the map opens. Use pooled marker objects, spatial indexing, and visibility filtering.

### 11.4 Player navigation

The map should support:

* Current player position.

* Current region.

* Compass direction.

* Destination pins.

* Custom player markers.

* Quest destinations.

* Known roads and routes.

* Fast-travel locations where already supported by game design.

* Route distance estimates.

* Elevation and terrain warnings.

A route should not be considered valid merely because two points are close together on the map. Navigation must understand blocked terrain and actual connections.

### 12\. Surface World & Underworld Integration

Aethwyrd already describes five canonical underground layers. V10 should treat them as a connected geographical system, not as five unrelated menus.

